  "configuration_id": "config_002",
  "reason": "Evidence: Our only strong baseline is config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) at 48.5% acc, while image-only is very weak (20.8%). We have only 2 data points, so uncertainty is high across all design axes. Question tested: config_002 changes exactly one factor from config_001—switching from singleprofile to multiprofile with avgpool aggregation—while keeping the same PCA encoder. This cleanly isolates whether capturing the distribution of cell states (multiprofile) adds signal beyond a single representative profile. Why it's the best next experiment: (1) It is a minimal, single-factor comparison to our best baseline, making the result easy to interpret. (2) The multiprofile decision is a foundational axis that affects all downstream multimodal configurations (configs 018–051 all require an aggregation strategy). If multiprofile+avgpool helps, it validates investing in more complex aggregations (transformer) and multimodal multiprofile variants. If it doesn't help, we can prune the multiprofile branch and focus budget on RNA encoder improvements. (3) avgpool is the simplest aggregation, so a negative result would implicate the multiprofile representation itself rather than aggregation complexity, reducing uncertainty efficiently under a tight budget."
}

